            <h3>Cost Delta After Adaptive Thinking</h3>
            <p>Sonnet 4.6 and Opus 4.8 ship with <strong>adaptive thinking</strong> mode enabled by default. This is a major behavioral shift from the original 4-line: the model now generates internal reasoning tokens before each assistant turn. Anthropic counts these reasoning tokens in the output token total.</p>

            <p>Empirical data from Anthropic's migration whitepaper and the APIRank team's production traffic (3.2M requests over April-May 2026):</p>

            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Workload Type</th><th>Avg Output Growth on 4.6 vs 4</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>Short Q&amp;A (&lt;200 output tokens)</td><td>+18%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Long-form writing</td><td>+24%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Code generation</td><td>+31%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Multi-step reasoning &#47; agentic</td><td>+38%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Function-calling only</td><td>+5% (minimal thinking needed)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p>For a coding-heavy workload that produced an average of 800 output tokens per call on Sonnet 4, expect ~1048 output tokens on Sonnet 4.6. At $15/M output, that's $0.0157 vs $0.012 per call — a 31% per-request cost increase despite identical published pricing. Multiply by your monthly request volume and the bill change is meaningful.</p>

            <p>If adaptive thinking is undesirable for a specific workload (e.g. low-latency user-facing chatbot where every token matters), Anthropic lets you disable it via the <code>thinking</code> request parameter — but you'll lose the +6 point benchmark gain.</p>

            <h2>Migration Audit Script (Python, 40 lines)</h2>
            <p>Before flipping any model strings, you need to know how many callsites reference the retired IDs. This script walks a directory tree, counts every match, and prints the file paths. It catches hardcoded model strings, environment variable defaults, and YAML/JSON config files — not just <code>.py</code>.</p>

            <h3>audit_retired_models.py</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-python">#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Find every callsite referencing a retired Anthropic model ID.

Usage: python3 audit_retired_models.py /path/to/your/repo
Outputs a table of (file, line, match) tuples and a per-model summary.
"""
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

RETIRED = [
    "claude-sonnet-4-20250514",
    "claude-opus-4-20250514",
    "claude-sonnet-4-5",
    "claude-opus-4-5",
]

# Match inside double-quoted strings, single-quoted strings, env-var defaults,
# and YAML/JSON values. Skips .git, node_modules, __pycache__, dist, build.
SKIP_DIRS = &#123;".git", "node_modules", "__pycache__", "dist", "build", ".venv"&#125;
SCAN_EXT = &#123;".py", ".js", ".ts", ".tsx", ".jsx", ".go", ".rb", ".java",
            ".yaml", ".yml", ".json", ".toml", ".env", ".sh", ".md"&#125;

def scan(root: Path) -> list[tuple[Path, int, str]]:
    hits = []
    for path in root.rglob("*"):
        if not path.is_file() or path.suffix not in SCAN_EXT:
            continue
        if any(part in SKIP_DIRS for part in path.parts):
            continue
        try:
            text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="ignore")
        except OSError:
            continue
        for i, line in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
            for model in RETIRED:
                if model in line:
                    hits.append((path, i, line.strip()[:120]))
    return hits

if __name__ == "__main__":
    root = Path(sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) &gt; 1 else ".")
    hits = scan(root)
    if not hits:
        print(f"✅ No retired Anthropic model IDs found under &#123;root&#125;")
        sys.exit(0)
    print(f"⚠️  Found &#123;len(hits)&#125; callsite(s) referencing retired models:\n")
    counts = &#123;m: 0 for m in RETIRED&#125;
    for path, lineno, snippet in hits:
        for m in RETIRED:
            if m in snippet:
                counts[m] += 1
        rel = path.relative_to(root)
        print(f"  &#123;rel&#125;:&#123;lineno&#125;  &#123;snippet&#125;")
    print(f"\nSummary:")
    for m, c in counts.items():
        if c: print(f"  &#123;m&#125;: &#123;c&#125;")
    sys.exit(1)  # non-zero so CI catches it</code></pre>

