            <h2>Migration Playbook (Tested on 2 Production Workloads)</h2>
            <p>The order of operations matters. Skipping any step risks either burning budget on unverified replacements or shipping a regression to production traffic.</p>

            <ol>
              <li><strong>Run the audit script</strong> — generate the callsite inventory above. Tag every hit with a <code>migration:s4-to-s46</code> or <code>migration:o4-to-o48</code> GitHub label.</li>
              <li><strong>Update non-production first</strong> — staging, dev, and CI get the new model IDs. Run your full test suite against Sonnet 4.6 / Opus 4.8. Reasoning outputs will differ in style; expect to update 2-3 prompt templates that hardcoded "concise" or "short" patterns.</li>
              <li><strong>Run a 1% canary</strong> — point 1% of production traffic at the new model for 24 hours. Compare p50 / p95 latency, error rate, and cost per request against the baseline. The script below does the cost calculation in real time.</li>
              <li><strong>Flip the routing layer</strong> — for applications using a model-routing abstraction (LiteLLM, Portkey, or your own wrapper), update the routing config and roll forward. For hardcoded callsites, ship the code change behind a feature flag and ramp 10% &rarr; 50% &rarr; 100% over 3 days.</li>
              <li><strong>Set a token budget alert</strong> — the +25% output token growth on adaptive thinking will silently inflate your bill. Set an alert at 110% of your pre-migration monthly spend to catch runaway growth early.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Canary Cost Tracker</h3>
            <pre><code class="language-python">"""Track per-request cost during a 4-to-4.6/4.8 canary.

Reads Anthropic usage headers from each response, compares against the
pre-migration baseline (Sonnet 4 at $3/$15 per MTok), and prints a
running summary. Drop into your existing load-test or canary harness.
"""
import os
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass

PRICING = &#123;
    "claude-sonnet-4-20250514": (3.0, 15.0),   # baseline (retired)
    "claude-sonnet-4-6":        (3.0, 15.0),   # replacement
    "claude-opus-4-20250514":   (15.0, 75.0),  # baseline (retired)
    "claude-opus-4-8":          (5.0, 25.0),   # replacement
&#125;

@dataclass
class Usage:
    model: str
    input_tokens: int
    output_tokens: int

def cost(u: Usage) -> float:
    inp, out = PRICING[u.model]
    return (u.input_tokens * inp + u.output_tokens * out) / 1_000_000

def parse_anthropic_headers(headers: dict) -> Usage:
    """Parse x-anthropic-* usage headers from a response."""
    return Usage(
        model=headers.get("x-anthropic-model", "unknown"),
        input_tokens=int(headers.get("x-anthropic-input-tokens", 0)),
        output_tokens=int(headers.get("x-anthropic-output-tokens", 0)),
    )

# Example: feed live canary responses through this loop
total_cost = 0.0
n = 0
while canary_running:
    response_headers = wait_for_canary_response()
    usage = parse_anthropic_headers(response_headers)
    c = cost(usage)
    total_cost += c
    n += 1
    if n % 100 == 0:
        print(f"[&#123;n:5d&#125;] avg cost/call $&#123;total_cost/n:.5f&#125;  model=&#123;usage.model&#125;")
    time.sleep(0.01)</code></pre>

            <h2>FAQ</h2>
            <p><strong>Q: What happened to Claude Sonnet 4 and Opus 4 on June 15, 2026?</strong></p>
