            <p><strong>Q: Which Anthropic model should I migrate Sonnet 4 to?</strong></p>
            <p>A: Sonnet 4.6 is the drop-in replacement at the same price. For coding-heavy workloads where benchmark gains matter, upgrade to Opus 4.8 ($5/$25) or Fable 5 ($10/$50, gated by export controls). The audit script helps you decide based on which workloads actually use Opus 4 today.</p>

            <p><strong>Q: Are Sonnet 4.6 and Opus 4.8 still available, or are they retiring soon?</strong></p>
            <p>A: Both are current production models as of June 26, 2026. No retirement date has been announced. Fable 5 will likely replace the 4.x line over the next 12 months, but that's speculation — Anthropic has not published a sunset date.</p>

            <p><strong>Q: Do token counts change between Sonnet 4 and Sonnet 4.6?</strong></p>
            <p>A: Per-token price is identical, but adaptive thinking produces 20-30% more output tokens on average. Budget for ~25% output growth on coding workloads.</p>

            <p><strong>Q: What is the cheapest way to test the migration before flipping production traffic?</strong></p>
            <p>A: Run a 1% canary on Sonnet 4.6 / Opus 4.8 for 24 hours. The cost tracker script above compares per-request cost and latency against your baseline.</p>

            <p><strong>Q: Can I keep using <code>claude-sonnet-4</code> as a string alias and have it auto-redirect?</strong></p>
            <p>A: No. Anthropic does not auto-redirect retired IDs. The API returns 404 immediately. You must change the model string in your code or routing layer.</p>

            <p><strong>Q: Does the June 12 export-control directive affect Sonnet 4.6 and Opus 4.8?</strong></p>
            <p>A: No. The export controls apply only to Fable 5 and Mythos 5. Sonnet 4.6 and Opus 4.8 remain accessible to all international customers, including regions where Fable 5 access is suspended.</p>

